<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andalusia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Andalusian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andalusia is a region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, whose name derives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Its cities, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, were centres of Islamic culture and power. It was a core territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, whose rulers built monuments there, before it fell largely to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in the thirteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andalusia.jpg" alt="Andalusia" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miguillen This picture was made for the Taller de Heráldica y vexilología of Spanish Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Andaluc%C3%ADa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The conquests of the thirteenth century transferred vast estates in <b>Andalusia</b> to the crown, the nobility, the Church and the military orders of Santiago, Calatrava and Alcántara, laying the foundations of a powerful landed aristocracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…and other southern French lords, he took Zaragoza after a siege of about seven months in 1118, defeated an Almoravid army at the Battle of Cutanda in 1120 and in 1125–1126 led a raid deep into <b>Andalusia</b>, returning with thousands of Mozarab Christians to settle his new lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Alfonso VII recovered the offensive, campaigned into <b>Andalusia</b> and took Almería in 1147 with Genoese and Catalan help, though it was lost in 1157.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Abbasid government from its foundation, New Persian eventually rivalled Arabic as a language of administration and high culture across the eastern Islamic world, and Sasanian motifs such as the simurgh, the royal hunt and the enthroned audience scene permeated Islamic decorative arts from <b>Andalusia</b> to Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The bathing culture of <b>Andalusia</b> continues Romano-Visigothic practice; Reccopolis and Toledo show heavy Byzantine architectural influence, and the votive crowns of the treasure of Guarrazar, buried during the Muslim invasion, survive from treasures that amazed the conquerors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They had close contact with the Greek colonies of Emporion, Rhode and Hemeroskopeion and with the Phoenicians of southern <b>Andalusia</b>, whose first colony in the peninsula, Gadir (Roman Gades, modern Cádiz), was founded in 1100 BCE, followed by Malaka, Sexi and Abdera.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
